<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day31" xml:id="day-31-lecture">
    <title>Day 31</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Ch 12.1, 12.4: Unsupervised Learning &amp; Clustering
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <objectives>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>Distinguish between supervised and unsupervised learning.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>Explain the goals of clustering methods.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>Interpret a dendrogram produced by hierarchical clustering.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>Define and distinguish different linkage methods.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>Perform hierarchical clustering in Python.</p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Supervised vs. Unsupervised Learning</title>
    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        <stack>
            <p>Supervised Learning</p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>Input data and corresponding output values are available.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Includes regression and classification.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Compare predictions, <m>\hat{Y}</m>, with observed outputs, <m>Y</m>.</p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </stack>
        <stack>
            <p>Unsupervised Learning</p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>No predefined output values.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Focuses on relationships among predictors or samples.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Often used for exploratory data analysis.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Goals and evaluation criteria are often less well-defined and more subjective.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>No universally accepted assessment measure.</p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </stack>
    </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Examples of Unsupervised Learning Problems</title>
    <p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>Gene expression analysis: Identify subgroups of patients with breast cancer who have similar gene expression profiles.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>Online shopping: Group shoppers with similar browsing and purchase histories to recommend related products.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>Search engines: Identify relationships among search queries and results to help select relevant results.</p>
        </li>
    </ul>
    </p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Big Idea: Clustering</title>

            <image source="figures/sphx_glr_plot_cluster_comparison_001.png" width="50%">
            <shortdescription>Comparison of different clustering methods and the clusters they identify in a data set.</shortdescription>
        </image>

            <p>Clustering: Relationships among samples</p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>Find subgroups or clusters within a data set.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Sometimes specify the desired number of clusters in advance.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Many different clustering techniques are available.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Must define what makes two data points similar or different.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>


</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Dendrogram</title>
    <image source="figures/Fig12-12.png" width="80%">
        <shortdescription>Dendrogram illustrating the hierarchical merging of individual data points into larger clusters.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>Bottom-up agglomeration: Start with each point as its own cluster and progressively merge clusters.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>The horizontal ordering of the leaves is arbitrary and can be rearranged.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>R draws leaves at equal heights rather than at zero.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Warning: This is not an example of single linkage.</p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
    <title>A Bigger Example</title>
    <sidebyside widths="60% 35%">
        <stack>
            <image source="figures/Fig12-10.png" width="100%">
                <shortdescription>Scatterplot of simulated data generated from three known groups, shown with different colors.</shortdescription>
            </image>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>Data were generated with three known group labels.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Pretend the colors are hidden: Can clustering recover the groups?</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </stack>
        <image source="figures/Fig12-11a.png" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>Cluster visualization of the same data, illustrating the groups identified by a clustering method.</shortdescription>
        </image>
    </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Single Linkage</title>
    <p>Distance between clusters \(A\) and \(B\): the smallest distance between any pair of points, one from each cluster.</p>
    <p>
        <md>
            L(A,B) = \min_{a \in A,\; b \in B} \|a-b\|
        </md>
    </p>
    <sidebyside widths="30% 30%">
        <image source="figures/SingleLink-0p0.png" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>Illustration of single linkage between two clusters.</shortdescription>
        </image>
        <image source="figures/SingleLink-Distance.png" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>Illustration of the minimum distance between points in two clusters under single linkage.</shortdescription>
        </image>
    </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

