<paragraphs>
    <title>Linkage Methods</title>
<table xml:id="table-linkage-methods">
    <title>Common Linkage Methods</title>
    <tabular>
        <col width="20%"/>
        <col width="80%"/>
        <row>
            <cell>Linkage</cell>
            <cell>Description</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Complete</cell>
            <cell>
                Maximal intercluster dissimilarity. Compute all pairwise dissimilarities between the observations in cluster <m>A</m> and the observations in cluster <m>B</m>, and record the largest of these dissimilarities.
            </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Single</cell>
            <cell>
                Minimal intercluster dissimilarity. Compute all pairwise dissimilarities between the observations in cluster <m>A</m> and the observations in cluster <m>B</m>, and record the smallest of these dissimilarities. Single linkage can result in extended, trailing clusters in which single observations are fused one at a time.
            </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Average</cell>
            <cell>
                Mean intercluster dissimilarity. Compute all pairwise dissimilarities between the observations in cluster <m>A</m> and the observations in cluster <m>B</m>, and record the average of these dissimilarities.
            </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Centroid</cell>
            <cell>
                Dissimilarity between the centroid for cluster <m>A</m> (a mean vector of length <m>p</m>) and the centroid for cluster <m>B</m>. Centroid linkage can result in undesirable inversions.
            </cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>
</table>

            <p>How do we define the distance between two clusters?</p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>Four common approaches are shown at left.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Average and complete linkage tend to produce more balanced dendrograms than single linkage.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Centroid linkage is used in applications such as genomics.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Centroid linkage can produce an inversion: two clusters merge at a height below the height of either individual cluster.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>

</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Example with Complete Linkage</title>
    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        <stack>
            <image source="figures/Fig12-12a.png" width="50%">
                <shortdescription>Illustration of complete linkage between two clusters.</shortdescription>
            </image>
            <p>Distance between clusters \(A\) and \(B\): the largest distance between any pair of points, one from each cluster.</p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    L(A,B) = \max_{a \in A,\; b \in B} \|a-b\|
                </md>
            </p>
        </stack>
        <image source="figures/Fig12-13.png" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>Dendrogram produced using complete linkage.</shortdescription>
        </image>
    </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Examples of Different Linkage Methods</title>
    <image source="figures/Fig12-14.png" width="70%">
        <shortdescription>Comparison of hierarchical clustering results obtained using different linkage methods.</shortdescription>
    </image>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Dependence on the Dissimilarity Measure</title>
    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        <stack>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>The choice of dissimilarity measure depends on the application and context.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Clustering results can change substantially with the chosen distance measure.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Each distance measure encodes assumptions about the geometry and shape of the underlying space.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </stack>
        <stack>
            <image source="figures/Clustering-DistanceTweet-JustFig.jpeg" width="100%">
                <shortdescription>Illustration of how different distance measures can produce different notions of similarity and geometric relationships.</shortdescription>
            </image>
            <p>Photo credit: <url href="https://twitter.com/UsmanKayaniPhD/status/1518243229832994818">Usman Kayani</url></p>
        </stack>
    </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>



    </handout>